# Finite-dimensional parabolic cohomology for Gamma1(N)

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.parabolicH1_finiteDimensional`
(`c14170fd-66a7-4019-a607-ed4295c3c4e3`), by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-07.

Proof: submission `68146ab0-259d-482c-8a19-302f07939975`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-07 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

Let $N$ be a positive integer and $n\ge 0$. Let $V_n$ be the complex space of homogeneous binary
polynomials of degree $n$, with the MTT left substitution action of $\Gamma_1(N)$. Then

$$\dim_{\mathbf C} H^1_{\mathrm{par}}(\Gamma_1(N),V_n)<\infty.$$

Here parabolic cohomology is formed from the ordinary one-cocycles whose restriction to each cyclic
cusp stabilizer is principal, modulo all principal coboundaries. This is the finiteness input needed
to use dimension comparisons in the Eichler–Shimura surjectivity argument; it includes $n=0$ and all
positive levels.

## Explanation of the source proof

# Finite-dimensional parabolic cohomology

This is a direct proof, not a conditional reduction. It imports only the
parabolic-cohomology definitions and mathlib, with no platform theorem.

For a representation with finite-dimensional coefficients, a one-cocycle is
determined by its values on any group generating set. This is proved by subgroup
closure induction: multiplication follows from the cocycle equation; inversion
follows by evaluating the equation on the inverse times the original element.
Restriction to a finite generating set is therefore an injective linear map
into a finite product of the coefficient space.

Mathlib proves that SL2(Z) is generated by S and T. Gamma1(N) has finite index
when N is positive, so Schreier's theorem gives finite generation. Mathlib also
proves that the homogeneous polynomial submodule of any fixed degree in finitely
many variables is finitely generated. Thus the MTT symmetric-power coefficient
space is finite-dimensional, as is its space of one-cocycles. The parabolic
cocycles form a linear subspace, and parabolic cohomology is a quotient of that
subspace. Both operations preserve finite-dimensionality.

The proof includes degree zero and levels one and two, requires no torsion-free
hypothesis, and uses no formula for cusp-form dimensions. The coefficient action
and parabolic quotient are the shared platform definition MTT_ParabolicCohomology.
Its substitution action calculations credit allychan327's accepted reflection
proof b65c560e-ff07-4802-bc88-856b821148e6.

Source for the mathematical objects: Columbia Spring 2021 seminar notes,
https://www.math.columbia.edu/~dmarcil/Seminars/2021_Spring/Notes/Week4-5.pdf,
section 1.1, pages 1–3. The source presents a finite cellular model; the proof
here uses the elementary finite-generation argument above instead.

